  "role_correction_note": "Correct Japanese terms for paired weapons kata roles (confirmed via search, e.g. Clarkson AikidoCU weapons glossary and Wikipedia's Shidachi article): UCHIDACHI (\u6253\u592a\u5200, 'striking sword') = the one who makes the first attack in a given exchange/kata -- traditionally the senior/teaching role, feeding true attacks. SHIDACHI (\u4ed5\u592a\u5200, 'doing sword') = the one who receives and executes the technique in response -- traditionally the role being taught. Unlike uke/tori in a throwing technique, the exchange within a kata is back-and-forth (both partners strike and parry in turn), but the uchidachi/shidachi ASSIGNMENT itself is still fixed for a given performance of the kata -- it's not meaningless the way 'tori'/'uke' would be if forced onto this structure, it's just a different kind of role than a grab-and-throw technique has. Renamed partner_a/partner_b to uchidachi/shidachi below, but the assignment (which was which screen-position crop) is UNVERIFIED -- same caveat as before: confirm by watching who initiates each strike in the kata, don't assume from screen position.",
  "scope_confirmation": "CONFIRMED IN SCOPE: 'Jo and Jo: Shomenuchi, Yokomenuchi, Yokobarai, Douchi, Ashiuchi' appears word-for-word in Johan's own 3Kyu.odt (Kumiwaza/Tsuzukiwaza section), matching toitsu.de exactly. This clip is directly usable reference material for the 3rd kyu handout.",
  "role_verification_attempt": "Partial verification against the footage: at the kata's opening (frames ~0004, 0006 in jobokken_scan), the LEFT-side figure (mapped to 'uchidachi' below) is the one raising the jo into a striking position while the RIGHT-side figure ('shidachi') holds a lower, more receiving stance -- consistent with left=uchidachi initiating the first strike (Shomenuchi, per toitsu.de's 5-strike sequence for this kata). However, a later sampled frame (~0026, roughly the Yokobarai/Douchi pair) shows the RIGHT-side figure's jo more extended/forward, which could mean genuine role alternation between strike-pairs, or could just be shidachi's parry/counter-position at that instant -- single coarse-interval frames can't distinguish attack-instant from response-instant reliably. Moderate confidence in the left=uchidachi assignment for the kata's opening, low confidence that it holds uniformly across all five strike-pairs without watching the continuous motion.",
  "hakama_caveat": "HAKAMA CAVEAT (applies to every clip in this set): all source footage shows practitioners wearing hakama (wide pleated pants) that fully obscure real leg shape. MediaPipe's confidence score does not flag this as a problem -- it confidently fits to the visible fabric silhouette, not the true limb underneath, so a high/unflagged confidence on a knee or ankle landmark does NOT mean the estimate is trustworthy the way it would for an unobscured joint. This is distinct from (and not caught by) the existing low_confidence_landmarks flagging, which is about detection confidence, not fabric-occlusion accuracy. Practical implication: trust arm/upper-body bone_directions more than leg ones from this data; for stance width and foot position specifically, check the source frame images directly rather than the numeric leg angles. NOTE: this is a non-issue for the Godot tool's own visual output -- the build spec explicitly specifies gi-less, clothing-free mannequins (see spec section 5.1 and 2.2), so hakama never needs to be rendered. It only matters for how much to trust THIS pipeline's leg data when using it to hand-pose the mannequins."
}